{
  "cost_of_complete_deletion": "On D6 the common-column count per triple is far below the alignment length, because 15 of 16 sequences carry a gap block. That lost power is the price of removing the confound, and it is why complete deletion should be the default only when there is missing data to delete.",
  "criteria_corrected_after_a_first_run": {
    "corrections": [
      {
        "now": "The per-triple VERDICT is compared instead, and the statistic is the number of triples that pairwise deletion calls a violation and complete deletion does not. P5 adds the control that makes it meaningful: with no missing data the two policies must agree exactly.",
        "was": "P6 compared the largest MARGIN under pairwise deletion with the largest margin under complete deletion.",
        "wrong_because": "Complete deletion shrinks the denominator -- on D6 it leaves about 1100 of 4000 columns -- so one differing site is worth more and its margins are LARGER, even though it is strictly more conservative about what counts as a violation. The two numbers have different denominators and are no more comparable than the two pairwise distances that finding 6 is about."
      },
      {
        "now": "The gate is the seed-PAIRED strict inequality (P1): the same clonal tree with a recombinant added must give a larger margin than without it. The 2x result is kept as an ungated finding (P1b) rather than dropped, because its failure at 2 of 5 seeds is itself the result.",
        "was": "The power gate required the positive control's margin to exceed TWICE the clonal floor, the threshold v1.6.0 used.",
        "wrong_because": "The floor is a maximum over 560 triples, an extreme-value statistic, and this run shows it varying from 18/4000 to 39/4000 across five seeds. A 2x threshold on a ratio whose denominator moves by 2.2x is not a stable criterion, and v1.6.0's reported 3.389x was the best of the five seeds, not a typical value."
      }
    ],
    "why_this_block_exists": "A first run of this experiment used two criteria that were wrong, and both are recorded here rather than quietly replaced, because a criterion changed after seeing data is the easiest way to launder a negative result."
  },
