  "design": {
    "margins_are_exact_rationals": true,
    "null_nesting_theorem": "ANSE.GeneticaReticulata.fourPoint_of_ultrametric -- every symmetric ultrametric satisfies the four-point condition, so a quartet certificate refutes strictly more than a triple one.",
    "parameters": {
      "alignment_length": 4000,
      "gap_block_fraction": 0.25,
      "generations": 4,
      "mu_per_generation": 0.004,
      "relaxed_clock_spread": 1.0,
      "saturated_mu": 0.04,
      "seeds": [
        2026,
        2027,
        2028,
        2029,
        2030
      ]
    },
    "reference_dataset": "D1_clonal_strict_clock. Every comparison is SEED-PAIRED against D1 at the same seed and decided by an exact integer cross-product.",
    "why_several_seeds": "A single-seed margin is not a result. Each prediction reports the number of seeds at which it held, not an average of margins."
  },
  "experiment": "Does the four-point (tree-level) test separate reticulation from rate variation, where the ultrametric (clock-level) test cannot? And does complete deletion within the subset remove the gap confound of the audit's finding 6?",
  "preregistered_predictions": [
    {
      "claim": "POWER CONTROL, seed-paired. Adding one recombinant to a clonal tree raises the complete-deletion triple margin above the same tree's margin without it.",
      "criterion": "holds at all seeds",
      "gated": true,
      "held": true,
      "id": "P1",
      "observed": "5/5"
    },
    {
      "claim": "FINDING. The same comparison at v1.6.0's threshold: D3's margin exceeds 2x D1's.",
      "criterion": "reported, not gated",
      "gated": false,
      "id": "P1b",
      "observed": "3/5",
      "reading": "Where this fails, the triple statistic does not clear its own published threshold. The clonal floor is a maximum over triples and varies by more than 2x across seeds, so this threshold cannot be relied on."
    },
    {
      "claim": "A relaxed clock on a purely clonal tree breaks ULTRAMETRICITY: D2's complete-triple margin exceeds 2x D1's at the same seed.",
      "criterion": "reported, not gated",
      "divergence_matched": "The relaxed clock's rate multipliers are normalised to mean 1, so D2 has the same expected divergence as D1 and only the clock differs. Without that, a relaxed clock is also a faster clock and the result would confound rate variation with saturation.",
      "gated": false,
      "id": "P2",
      "observed_above_1x_paired": "4/5",
      "observed_above_2x": "3/5",
      "reading": "Where this holds, a triple certificate does not distinguish reticulation from rate variation -- risk 2 of docs/GENETICA_RETICULATA.md section 7, realised. Compare D2's per-seed margins against D3's directly: if they overlap, the statistic cannot separate the causes."
    },
    {
      "claim": "THE DISCRIMINATING PREDICTION. The same relaxed-clock data does NOT break the FOUR-POINT condition beyond the floor: D2's quartet margin does not exceed 2x D1's.",
      "criterion": "violations at <= 1 of 5 seeds",
      "gated": false,
      "held": true,
      "id": "P3",
      "observed_violations": "0/5",
      "reading": "A violation count of 0 or 1 means the four-point test is largely immune to the confound that defeats the triple test. A higher count means it is confounded too, and the method has no statistic that separates the two causes -- a negative result about this approach, reported rather than gated away. Read it next to P7: a relaxed clock also redistributes divergence among lineages, so some elevation may be local saturation rather than non-additivity."
    },
    {
      "claim": "POWER CONTROL for the four-point test. Reciprocal mosaics break it: D5's quartet margin exceeds 2x D1's quartet floor at the same seed.",
      "criterion": "holds at all seeds",
      "gated": true,
      "held": true,
      "id": "P4",
      "observed_above_1x_paired": "5/5",
      "observed_above_2x": "5/5",
      "why_reciprocal": "A single recombinant need not violate the four-point condition; the shape that does is a pair of reciprocal mosaics, each close to both parents. This is the configuration Lean's reciprocal_recombinants_witness proves non-vacuous."
    },
    {
      "claim": "CONTROL FOR THE POLICY COMPARISON. With no missing data, complete deletion within the triple IS pairwise deletion, so the two policies must agree on every one of D1's 560 triples.",
      "criterion": "holds at all seeds",
      "gated": true,
      "held": true,
      "id": "P5b",
      "observed": "5/5",
      "why": "Without this, a non-zero manufactured count on gappy data could be an artefact of the comparison itself."
    },
    {
      "claim": "THE FIX BITES, AND FINDING 6 IS REAL. On gap-blocked but strictly clonal data with no reticulation, pairwise deletion calls triples violations that complete deletion within the same triple does not.",
      "criterion": "at least one manufactured triple at >= 4 of 5 seeds",
      "gated": true,
      "held": true,
      "id": "P6",
      "observed": "5/5",
      "reading": "Every manufactured triple is a kernel-acceptable certificate about a genealogy that is exactly a clock tree. The count is the size of the confound; complete deletion is what removes it."
    },
    {
      "claim": "EXPLORATORY. Saturation alone breaks additivity, so a high-divergence clonal population may lift the quartet margin even with no reticulation and a strict clock.",
      "criterion": "reported, not gated",
      "gated": false,
      "id": "P7",
      "observed": "5/5",
      "reading": "A four-point violation at high divergence is a p-distance artefact, not reticulation. If this fires, the method needs a divergence ceiling or a corrected distance, and the limitation must be stated."
    },
    {
      "claim": "SECONDARY. A single recombinant need not violate the four-point condition, so D3's and D4's quartet margins may stay at the floor while their triple margins do not.",
      "criterion": "reported, not gated",
